From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CBOC-Vita <344212538+CBOC-Vita@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:07:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 69/70] gl: the alpha test is dropped whenever its comparison
 passes for every alpha the draw's fragments can have

The first rule dropped the test (and so the shader's `discard`, which on a tile-based GPU keeps the hardware
from settling visibility before shading) only when every fragment had alpha 255. The decision is now an interval
rule in host/gpu/gl/gl_alpha_test.hpp: the 8-bit alpha range of the draw's fragments from the vertex colours or
material (min and max over the vertices), the lighting's ambient alpha, and the texture function (modulate, blend, add
multiply the texel's alpha, replace takes it, decal and a texture whose alpha is unused leave the colour's) with what is
known of the texture (every texel opaque), against the comparison and reference. A guard of two units is applied
wherever a bound is not exactly 0 or 255. A constant fade of an opaque texture, a lit draw at a constant ambient
alpha and "replace" with an opaque texture now lose the test; a texture with any alpha below 255 keeps it.

The texture scan that says a texture is opaque used to happen only with gfx.texture_filter or gfx.mipmaps on, so with
both off almost no draw lost its test (1 a frame instead of 409): it is made when a 3D draw first needs it.

gl_alpha_elide_range=0 returns to the first rule, gl_alpha_elide=0 keeps every test. GlStats::alpha_tested/alpha_elided
and the [perf] line ("alpha-test draws N, without discard M") count draws. portablekit_alpha_test_tests checks the
decision table against a brute-force evaluation of every alpha.

Mission-1 dumps (100 flips, software GL, ui upscale budget off so the replay is deterministic): 100/100 pictures
byte-identical with the test kept and dropped, in four configurations (defaults, all options off, FXAA on, first rule).
Draws that lose the test: 350 -> 410 of 640 alpha-tested draws a frame; their covered area 46 % -> 71 %.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
